WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:13.000
So today, we're going to continue our discussion on computer vision. In particular, last week,

00:13.000 --> 00:22.220
we talked about the mathematical transformation that allows us to map points in a 3D world

00:22.220 --> 00:30.580
onto a camera image. And most importantly, how to leverage that mathematical mapping

00:30.580 --> 00:39.020
in order to reconstruct the environment in 3D once we have access to 2D images. Now,

00:39.020 --> 00:43.380
this is a very important process because one of the most fundamental tasks that you want

00:43.380 --> 00:50.820
to achieve with a vision sensor is that of reconstructing the environment. But that is

00:50.820 --> 00:55.660
only one of the tasks that you want to achieve with a vision sensor. There are many more.

00:55.660 --> 01:02.420
And in particular, through vision, you are interested in detecting objects that can be

01:02.420 --> 01:10.020
used in order to reason semantically about environment. In particular, is a blob a human

01:10.020 --> 01:16.260
or an object? And what are the implications in terms of decision making? So in order to

01:16.260 --> 01:24.220
do so, we have to dig a little bit deeper about how to extract information from images.

01:24.220 --> 01:31.940
In particular, the topic of today is to learn how to, first of all, filter images in order

01:31.940 --> 01:38.300
to remove, for example, noise that might affect the information that we extract from an image.

01:38.300 --> 01:45.620
And second, how to detect and describe key features of an image such as a corner, edges,

01:45.620 --> 01:51.580
and so on. On Thursday, we'll see how to use this information in order to reason at

01:51.580 --> 01:57.340
a higher level about the reconstruction of environment, like how do we start extracting

01:57.340 --> 02:02.260
lines that can be used for localization or how do we identify whether there is a person

02:02.260 --> 02:07.580
in an image and so on. So today is mostly a geometric understanding. On Thursday will

02:07.580 --> 02:16.700
be mostly a semantic understanding. Now, how do we represent images? Well, the

02:16.700 --> 02:23.260
raw output of an image sensor is a collection of pixels. But in practice, we want to describe

02:23.260 --> 02:28.900
the images in a more succinct way. And in particular, in terms of features that can

02:28.900 --> 02:37.100
be used, for example, to match points of interest across images and can be used in order to

02:37.140 --> 02:42.500
detect whether a certain part of an image corresponds to a person, an object, and so

02:42.500 --> 02:47.380
on and so forth. Typical features that you would like to extract are, for example, corners,

02:47.380 --> 02:54.980
edges, blobs, and so on and so forth. So for example, here in this image of a butterfly,

02:54.980 --> 03:00.060
I'm showing the typical output of an image detector. Well, you can see how the image

03:00.060 --> 03:07.980
detector in question is picking up a number of salient features or key points of the image

03:07.980 --> 03:14.620
that can be used later on in a downstream application in order to do semantic understanding.

03:14.620 --> 03:22.260
Now, an image processing pipeline, at the end of the day, consists of four main steps.

03:22.260 --> 03:30.380
The first step is the step of image filtering. Basically, we want to clean up or, in other

03:30.380 --> 03:39.380
words, modify the image in order to ease the downstream task of future detection, description,

03:39.380 --> 03:45.940
and later on, high-level processing. At the very least, we want, for example, to remove

03:45.940 --> 03:53.020
noise from an image. The second step is the step of detecting key

03:53.020 --> 03:59.500
features or equipment referred to as key points. As I said, corners, edges, and so on. They

03:59.500 --> 04:06.180
can be used across images for stereo correspondences or for structure from motion, or it can also

04:06.180 --> 04:14.700
be used in order to detect objects. The third step is a step of future description.

04:14.980 --> 04:21.780
Once we have detected a feature, we want to be able to describe it with photometric quantities,

04:21.780 --> 04:28.380
like for example, local gradients around it, in a way that allows us to match that feature

04:28.380 --> 04:33.740
with the same feature in another image. For example, we take an image and then we

04:33.740 --> 04:39.980
take another image from a different pose. The point is that if there is a corner that

04:39.980 --> 04:44.140
we want to track, we want to be able to establish a correspondence between that corner and the

04:44.140 --> 04:48.940
first image and the corner in the second image. To do so, we need to be able to describe

04:48.940 --> 04:54.180
locally what that image looks like in order to have a robust matching between the same

04:54.180 --> 05:01.300
feature and two different images. Make sense? Future detection means there is

05:01.300 --> 05:06.180
a corner. Future description means this corner has specific properties. For example, there

05:06.180 --> 05:11.700
is a lot of yellow around it, so that when I see another corner in another image with

05:11.700 --> 05:17.380
a lot of yellow around it, most likely those two corners are indeed the same, just imaged

05:17.380 --> 05:22.780
into different images. Based on that, then we can have matching pairs that can be used

05:22.780 --> 05:26.740
for a number of applications, for example, serial reconstruction.

05:26.740 --> 05:31.700
The fourth step, and this is the step that we'll look at on Thursday, is the one over

05:31.700 --> 05:37.060
high-level processing. Now that we're able to detect features, robustly track them across

05:37.060 --> 05:44.340
images, how do we use them for high-level processing such as localization or object

05:44.340 --> 05:50.620
detection and so on? Today we're going to focus on the first three steps, and on Thursday

05:50.620 --> 05:56.900
we're going to focus on the fourth step. Let's start then with the first step, which

05:56.900 --> 06:05.020
is the step of image processing that in a nutshell entails accepting or rejecting certain

06:05.060 --> 06:09.820
frequency components. If you're familiar with signal processing, this is very similar to

06:09.820 --> 06:17.180
what is done to signals, like for example, electromagnetic waves. You filter them out

06:17.180 --> 06:22.140
in order to keep some frequency components in order to, for example, remove noise or

06:22.140 --> 06:26.340
for example, make sure that within the same channel you can transmit multiple signals

06:26.340 --> 06:33.220
at different frequencies. The difference here though is that instead of dealing with a signal

06:33.220 --> 06:41.100
in time, we're dealing with an image. The idea of filtering practically corresponds

06:41.100 --> 06:47.980
to finding a new image whose pixels are a combination of the pixels of the previous

06:47.980 --> 06:55.740
image. In particular, the key idea is to represent an image as a two-dimensional function that

06:55.740 --> 07:04.500
maps row coordinates and pixel coordinates into intensity values. So for example, consider

07:04.500 --> 07:12.300
this image here, which is in grayscale. This image is a bunch of pixels. Each pixel is

07:12.300 --> 07:22.260
labeled by a row, referred to as i, and a column, referred to as j. And each pixel is

07:22.260 --> 07:29.660
stored in the computer with a number, which represents the gray level, typically between

07:29.660 --> 07:42.540
0 to 155. So 0 corresponds to black, 255 corresponds to white. If you have colors, then you should

07:42.540 --> 07:48.500
think of having three of these tables, one on top of each other, each one for the three

07:48.500 --> 07:55.100
fundamental colors, like red, blue, and so on. Any question about this? It's fundamental.

07:55.100 --> 08:01.820
So an image from the purposes of the image processing pipeline is represented as a two-dimensional

08:01.820 --> 08:11.660
function that maps each row, column, value into an integer that corresponds to the intensity

08:11.660 --> 08:18.340
value for that pixel. If you use grayscale, it would be a number between 0 and 255. Then

08:18.340 --> 08:26.340
with this interpretation, filtering corresponds to producing an output image that takes as

08:26.340 --> 08:32.340
an input image and applies some transformation of the pixels in the input image, according

08:32.340 --> 08:36.860
to some rules that I'm going to discuss in a second.

08:36.860 --> 08:47.060
All right. So filtering can be done in fundamentally two ways, either in the frequency domain or

08:47.100 --> 08:52.380
in the spatial domain. Again, if you're familiar with signal processing, you know that oftentimes

08:52.380 --> 08:57.020
filtering is done in the frequency domain, whereby you take the Fourier transform of

08:57.020 --> 09:03.380
your signal, then you operate in the Fourier domain, and then you transform back the signal

09:03.380 --> 09:09.180
to the time domain. If you're not familiar with the Fourier transforms, it's not a problem

09:09.180 --> 09:15.460
because in this class, actually, we're going to focus on filters in the spatial domain,

09:15.460 --> 09:18.300
meaning without leveraging Fourier transforms.

09:18.300 --> 09:25.340
Fourier transforms are indeed quite useful. For example, when I was working at JPL, I

09:25.340 --> 09:32.940
was doing a lot of image processing on the images taken by the orbiter Amaro around Mars.

09:32.940 --> 09:37.660
In order to make computation efficient, what I was doing was to take discrete Fourier transforms

09:37.660 --> 09:41.180
on the images, do all the image processing pipeline in the Fourier domain, which is much

09:41.180 --> 09:44.940
faster, and then convert it back into the spatial domain.

09:44.940 --> 09:49.020
But anyway, this is beyond the scope of this class. In this class, we're going to focus

09:49.020 --> 09:57.540
on spatial filters. Spatial filters are relatively simple, and they consist of two main ingredients.

09:57.540 --> 10:03.260
The first ingredient is the neighborhood of a pixel under examination, which I refer to

10:03.260 --> 10:10.860
here as S subscript of x, y. S of x, y is a neighborhood that for a given pixel with

10:10.860 --> 10:18.660
the coordinates x, y, associates a set of neighboring pixels. For example, this could

10:18.660 --> 10:26.180
be a rectangle with a three by three neighborhood. The pixel x, y itself, and then one pixel

10:26.180 --> 10:32.620
to the right, one pixel to the left, and these three on top, and these three at the bottom.

10:32.620 --> 10:44.860
The second step is then a predefined operation on the image pixels encompassed by that neighborhood.

10:44.860 --> 10:51.180
So for example, here, I'm taking this image as an input with some coins, I consider a

10:51.180 --> 11:01.060
rectangular neighborhood, and I consider as operation that of smoothing. So in particular,

11:01.060 --> 11:16.700
the operation S x, y entails... So say that S of x, y is a three by three neighborhood

11:16.700 --> 11:28.220
with the pixel with the coordinate x, y at the center. Then a possible operation is to

11:28.220 --> 11:34.620
multiply each one of the intensities of the neighborhood pixels, including x, y pixels

11:34.620 --> 11:41.460
by one, and then divide by nine, the size of the neighborhood. So what we're doing here

11:41.460 --> 11:47.780
is basically just taking the spatial average of the intensities around pixel x, y. So this

11:47.780 --> 11:54.420
corresponds to smoothing, and we'll see that more formally later. So the end result will

11:54.420 --> 12:01.540
be this image, which looks a bit blurred. So you might say, why on earth you buy an

12:01.540 --> 12:06.460
expensive camera in order to get an image that is very crisp, and then through image

12:06.460 --> 12:14.500
processing you get something that is blurred. You have an idea why that could be useful?

12:14.500 --> 12:22.060
Well yes, down sampling could be one reason. Exactly, remove noise. So as you will see,

12:22.060 --> 12:27.820
in order to detect features, like for example edges, you're trying to look at parts of an

12:27.820 --> 12:34.380
image where you have discontinuity in the intensities, which basically entail looking

12:34.380 --> 12:41.540
at the notion of a derivative for an image. But as you may know, taking a derivative amplifies

12:41.540 --> 12:46.020
noise. So before taking a derivative, you want to smooth out your input, for example

12:46.020 --> 12:57.900
with an average filter. Specifically, within the domain of spatial filters, spatial filters

12:57.900 --> 13:03.980
can be either linear or non-linear. Linear means that we perform an operation over the

13:03.980 --> 13:09.660
neighborhood of pixels that is linear. Non-linear means that we perform a non-linear operation.

13:09.660 --> 13:15.740
In this class, we focus on linear filters. So we focus on spatial linear filters. In

13:15.740 --> 13:21.460
particular, linear filters are filters that can be written as a correlation between the

13:21.460 --> 13:31.620
input image denoted as I and a mask or kernel or window denoted as F. So the notation could

13:31.620 --> 13:38.100
be a bit cumbersome, but the notion is very easy and I will give you an example in a second.

13:38.100 --> 13:47.220
So just to fix the nomenclature, capital N is specifically 2N plus 1 is the number

13:47.220 --> 13:54.220
of rows of the filter mask, and 2N plus 1 is the number of columns on the filter mask.

13:54.220 --> 14:05.500
The filter mask is basically another matrix that is smaller usually than your input image.

14:05.500 --> 14:12.340
For example, in the case of the example that I just gave you, it was the matrix three by

14:12.340 --> 14:22.020
three with all ones. So what that formula is saying is actually encoding the operation

14:22.020 --> 14:27.020
that I just mentioned before. You multiply each pixel in the neighborhood for the corresponding

14:27.020 --> 14:35.620
component in the mask, and then you sum them out. So it's an average. Basically, a correlation

14:35.620 --> 14:43.740
you can see as an average of the insensities in the pixel neighborhood. Of course, nobody

14:43.740 --> 14:49.380
tells you that you need to have all ones. You can give weights that are different, and

14:49.380 --> 14:54.340
we will see later on how different filters give different weights and how that corresponds

14:54.340 --> 15:01.180
to different operations. So just to make sure that everyone is clear with this concept,

15:01.180 --> 15:13.100
so let's consider this image where you have, say, one, two, one, two, one, two, one, two,

15:13.100 --> 15:35.860
one, two, one, two, one, one, one, one, two, one, two, one, one, one, one. So that will

15:35.860 --> 15:50.620
will be your input image, which we denote as i.

15:50.620 --> 15:57.860
Let's consider as a mask f, the mask 3 by 3,

15:57.860 --> 16:01.900
where all the numbers are 1.

16:01.900 --> 16:04.740
Which basically means that we consider a neighborhood that

16:04.740 --> 16:08.020
is just a 3 by 3 neighborhood, basically a rectangular

16:08.020 --> 16:09.220
neighborhood.

16:09.220 --> 16:12.900
So let's assume that the idea is to process each pixel one

16:12.900 --> 16:14.100
by one.

16:14.100 --> 16:17.820
Let's assume that we are processing this pixel here.

16:17.820 --> 16:24.340
So how is this pixel mapped into the output image y prime?

16:24.340 --> 16:26.340
So what's the value of this pixel?

16:29.460 --> 16:32.920
So this will be the pixel with the second row

16:32.960 --> 16:34.920
and the third column.

16:34.920 --> 16:37.480
So in the output image, what is the value

16:37.480 --> 16:41.120
of the pixel second row and third column?

16:41.120 --> 16:45.040
If we apply this mask, and we consider

16:45.040 --> 16:48.560
the process of correlation.

16:48.560 --> 16:49.400
What's that?

16:49.400 --> 16:50.640
Is it 13?

16:50.640 --> 16:53.480
So we have 2, 2, 2, 2.

16:53.480 --> 16:59.120
So this is 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.

16:59.160 --> 16:59.660
It's OK.

17:06.240 --> 17:14.600
So what is a bit awkward here is that by applying this mask,

17:14.600 --> 17:17.840
we are actually increasing the intensity uniformly.

17:17.840 --> 17:21.040
We'll see later on how actually the average filter should

17:21.040 --> 17:23.160
be normalized in order to make sure

17:23.160 --> 17:28.240
that if you apply it on a patch that has uniform intensity,

17:28.240 --> 17:31.160
you get the same intensity as an output.

17:31.160 --> 17:33.320
But this is just to give you an example of what

17:33.320 --> 17:36.000
mathematically this formula means.

17:39.240 --> 17:42.540
A natural equation is, what do you do at a boundary?

17:42.540 --> 17:48.920
So if you are here, say, you can think

17:48.920 --> 17:51.180
of applying this mask as a superimposing

17:51.180 --> 17:53.760
this mask on your image.

17:53.760 --> 17:59.760
And then multiply the corresponding components.

17:59.760 --> 18:01.920
So here, we're getting out.

18:01.920 --> 18:05.040
The neighborhood is getting out of the domain of the image.

18:05.040 --> 18:07.520
So what do we do?

18:07.520 --> 18:10.320
So what do we multiply this number here and this number

18:10.320 --> 18:13.960
here and this number here in terms of the corresponding

18:13.960 --> 18:16.680
input image i, since I don't have values for the input

18:16.680 --> 18:18.360
image i here?

18:18.360 --> 18:20.440
So this is a limit of a convention.

18:20.440 --> 18:23.400
In reality, the image is much larger than the mask.

18:23.520 --> 18:26.440
So what happens at the boundary is not that important.

18:26.440 --> 18:29.760
But you had to code something for your code to run.

18:29.760 --> 18:31.600
So you have a number of options.

18:31.600 --> 18:34.720
The easiest one, and the one that you would use in your pset,

18:34.720 --> 18:37.120
is just pad the image with zeros.

18:40.200 --> 18:43.900
So just a larger image so that your correlation operation

18:43.900 --> 18:48.100
is well-defined also along the boundaries.

18:48.100 --> 18:49.760
But you can do also other stuff.

18:49.760 --> 18:52.880
You could, for example, crop the image.

18:52.880 --> 18:59.720
So just consider this subimage here,

18:59.720 --> 19:01.400
and then work on the restricted image

19:01.400 --> 19:04.840
so that the boundary is well-defined.

19:04.840 --> 19:07.560
Or you can extend it by using some more defined notion

19:07.560 --> 19:09.200
of extrapolation.

19:09.200 --> 19:11.880
Or if you have a little bit more imagination,

19:11.880 --> 19:14.720
you can think of warping the image such

19:14.720 --> 19:22.280
that this boundary becomes attached to this boundary.

19:22.320 --> 19:23.440
Sometimes it might make sense.

19:23.440 --> 19:25.960
Basically, you would wrap it in this direction

19:25.960 --> 19:28.680
and in that direction.

19:28.680 --> 19:30.200
In reality, just padding with zeros

19:30.200 --> 19:32.560
is the more natural option.

19:32.560 --> 19:33.360
So it is clear.

19:33.360 --> 19:34.360
Just want to make sure it's clear.

19:34.360 --> 19:36.360
Because if it's not clear, then everything else

19:36.360 --> 19:39.600
is going to be very confusing.

19:43.480 --> 19:47.600
So the easiest way of thinking about correlation

19:47.640 --> 19:53.540
is that we have an image, which is represented

19:53.540 --> 19:54.760
as a two-dimensional function.

19:59.520 --> 20:02.280
And then we consider a mask.

20:11.420 --> 20:13.280
And then for each element of the mask,

20:13.280 --> 20:19.480
we multiply the corresponding elements of the image.

20:19.480 --> 20:22.920
And then we center the mask at each pixel of the input image.

20:22.920 --> 20:25.280
So basically, this is a moving mask

20:25.280 --> 20:29.840
that goes throughout, that is laid throughout the image.

20:29.840 --> 20:30.600
Yeah?

20:30.600 --> 20:33.840
So is this the conclusion?

20:33.840 --> 20:34.840
No.

20:34.840 --> 20:37.920
Convolution, as we will see in a second,

20:37.920 --> 20:40.640
is when instead of having a plus here,

20:40.640 --> 20:42.800
we have a minus, which in other words,

20:42.840 --> 20:46.040
corresponds to do a correlation with a mirrored version

20:46.040 --> 20:47.600
of the mask.

20:47.600 --> 20:48.840
It's a tiny detail.

20:48.840 --> 20:53.320
But it turns out that the convolution is still

20:53.320 --> 20:54.000
a linear filter.

20:54.000 --> 20:57.120
So it's still under the purview of this class.

20:57.120 --> 20:59.600
But it turns out that that slightly different definition

20:59.600 --> 21:02.160
of mathematical operation provides

21:02.160 --> 21:05.080
a very important property that is that of associativity

21:05.080 --> 21:06.720
that we're going to see in a second.

21:06.720 --> 21:08.040
But strictly speaking, this formulation

21:08.040 --> 21:09.600
is a formulation of correlation.

21:09.600 --> 21:14.240
All right.

21:14.240 --> 21:15.880
So let's look at some examples.

21:15.880 --> 21:20.520
First example is the moving average filter,

21:20.520 --> 21:26.000
whereby the output image has pixel values that

21:26.000 --> 21:30.280
represent the average values of the pixels

21:30.280 --> 21:32.960
as weighted by the mask.

21:32.960 --> 21:35.360
In particular, the mask in the case of 3 by 3

21:35.360 --> 21:37.440
is a mask with constant values, which

21:37.440 --> 21:39.200
is normalized in order to make sure

21:39.200 --> 21:41.360
that the background intensity stays the same.

21:41.360 --> 21:43.360
So if you apply this moving average filter

21:43.360 --> 21:46.080
to an image with a constant intensity,

21:46.080 --> 21:49.800
say all equal to 50, as an output, we still get 50.

21:49.800 --> 21:51.640
An image with all pixels equal to 50.

21:51.640 --> 21:56.160
So when you put an output value, do you just round it,

21:56.160 --> 21:57.960
or is it something the same?

21:57.960 --> 22:01.120
Well, the intensity are always rounded.

22:01.120 --> 22:04.040
Yeah.

22:04.040 --> 22:08.880
All right, so this is an important filter.

22:08.880 --> 22:12.400
But one may argue that it's a bit weird

22:12.400 --> 22:16.280
that when we take the average, we give the same weight

22:16.280 --> 22:19.840
to the pixels at the boundary of the neighborhood

22:19.840 --> 22:25.600
as we give to the pixel whereby this mask is centered at.

22:25.600 --> 22:32.240
So a more common smoothing mask is the so-called Gaussian

22:32.240 --> 22:37.520
smoothing mask, whereby the idea is to have, as a mask,

22:37.520 --> 22:41.200
something that gives more weight to the pixel where the mask is

22:41.200 --> 22:44.240
centered and less weight to the pixels that

22:44.240 --> 22:47.560
are far from the center in proportion

22:47.560 --> 22:50.720
to an underlying Gaussian distribution.

22:50.720 --> 22:53.640
So think of a two-dimensional Gaussian

22:53.640 --> 22:55.400
whose formula is given here.

22:55.400 --> 22:59.240
As usual, sigma is the standard deviation

22:59.240 --> 23:04.400
and controls how fat your distribution is.

23:04.400 --> 23:06.280
The Gaussian mask is simply obtained

23:06.280 --> 23:09.560
by taking the two-dimensional Gaussian distribution

23:09.560 --> 23:11.000
and sampling it.

23:11.000 --> 23:14.120
Like, for example, if you use sigma equal to 0.85,

23:14.120 --> 23:15.760
this is the Gaussian mask that you get,

23:15.760 --> 23:18.080
which is a very typical one.

23:18.080 --> 23:23.400
Changing sigma basically changes the values of these weights.

23:23.400 --> 23:27.960
But the intuition is the same, that you give more weight

23:27.960 --> 23:31.520
to the pixel where the mask is centered and less weight

23:31.520 --> 23:33.040
to the neighborhoods.

23:33.040 --> 23:35.720
As usual, the mask is normalized.

23:35.720 --> 23:37.880
If you sum all these numbers, you get 16.

23:37.880 --> 23:40.400
So when you divide by 16, you still get 1.

23:40.400 --> 23:43.480
In order to ensure that if you apply this mask to something

23:43.480 --> 23:45.400
that has a uniform intensity, you

23:45.400 --> 23:49.920
get as an output something with the same intensity value.

23:49.920 --> 23:53.400
Now, as we were mentioning before,

23:53.400 --> 23:56.640
one of the most fundamental operations in image processing

23:56.640 --> 23:58.600
is to take derivatives, which means

23:58.600 --> 24:01.480
that since derivatives, as discussed,

24:01.480 --> 24:06.000
might amplify noise, as we will see more explicit in a second,

24:06.000 --> 24:11.160
the first step is actually to smooth or to blur that image.

24:11.160 --> 24:15.040
The Gaussian mask is the most typical way of doing so.

24:15.040 --> 24:18.120
So pretty much in any image processing pipeline

24:18.120 --> 24:20.360
that uses classical techniques, as opposed

24:20.360 --> 24:23.200
to neural networks, that's the first preprocessing step.

24:27.640 --> 24:29.440
Any question about correlation?

24:29.440 --> 24:31.520
As I said, the concept is simple,

24:31.520 --> 24:34.000
but if you are confused about the notation,

24:34.000 --> 24:37.800
then everything else becomes confusing.

24:37.800 --> 24:40.880
In the p-set, I will ask you to do by hand

24:40.880 --> 24:45.320
a couple of correlations so that becomes super clear.

24:45.320 --> 24:45.960
Convolution.

24:45.960 --> 24:50.720
So convolution is another natural spatial filter.

24:50.720 --> 24:53.600
It's very similar to correlation.

24:53.600 --> 25:01.240
The only thing is that we replace the plus with the minus.

25:01.240 --> 25:14.560
So instead of weighting the, so if this is our xy,

25:14.560 --> 25:20.840
so instead of weighting, so this will be our ij.

25:24.040 --> 25:31.600
Well, instead of weighting this pixel by this pixel,

25:31.600 --> 25:37.200
we actually flip the way this weighting is done.

25:37.200 --> 25:40.400
Flip means mirrored.

25:40.400 --> 25:42.080
So it turns out, so this operation

25:42.080 --> 25:43.320
is conceptually the same.

25:43.320 --> 25:45.480
It's just that we are flipping the indices.

25:45.480 --> 25:49.800
It turns out that this operation has the associativity property,

25:49.800 --> 25:55.560
meaning that convolving a mask with the image input i

25:55.560 --> 25:59.520
and then convolving the resulting image with f

25:59.520 --> 26:04.160
is equal to first convolve the two masks, f and g,

26:04.160 --> 26:09.240
and then use the convolved mask to convolve it with i.

26:09.240 --> 26:12.040
The result is the same.

26:12.040 --> 26:13.240
So you might say, who cares?

26:13.240 --> 26:16.440
So it turns out that depending on the sequence with respect

26:16.440 --> 26:18.880
to which you carry out convolution,

26:18.880 --> 26:21.000
you might require less operations.

26:21.000 --> 26:24.400
So it turns faster.

26:24.400 --> 26:27.680
And in particular, when we try to detect the edges, what we do

26:27.680 --> 26:30.920
is to smooth the image and take a derivative.

26:30.920 --> 26:33.360
So instead of smoothing the image

26:33.360 --> 26:36.920
and then take the derivative of the smooth image, what you do

26:36.920 --> 26:40.040
is to take the derivative of the smoothing mask

26:40.040 --> 26:45.080
and then use that mask in order to detect an edge.

26:45.080 --> 26:46.800
We will see that in a few minutes.

26:48.880 --> 26:52.360
Yeah?

26:52.360 --> 26:55.120
The associativity property, why it's

26:55.120 --> 27:00.120
used so much in applications like machine learning?

27:00.120 --> 27:04.440
Well, in the case of an image processing application,

27:04.440 --> 27:07.680
it's one of the reasons, yeah.

27:07.680 --> 27:10.280
When you say discrete, is it a result of the image?

27:10.280 --> 27:14.720
We were going to define that formally.

27:14.720 --> 27:18.400
Of course, since the image is a discrete function,

27:18.440 --> 27:22.000
whereby the domain is discrete and the output is discrete,

27:22.000 --> 27:24.280
the derivative cannot be defined in the same way that

27:24.280 --> 27:26.960
is defined in calculus, because the notion of limit

27:26.960 --> 27:29.920
for the increment that tends to 0 is not well-defined.

27:29.920 --> 27:33.040
So we will consider a discrete definition for the derivative.

27:33.040 --> 27:34.840
I guess the question I was more thinking about

27:34.840 --> 27:36.760
is the fact that there's both an x and a y dimension,

27:36.760 --> 27:39.040
so it don't need like a derivative or a gradient?

27:39.040 --> 27:39.960
Oh, yeah, sure.

27:39.960 --> 27:40.560
Of course.

27:40.560 --> 27:41.280
Yeah.

27:41.280 --> 27:43.600
That concept carries forward here,

27:43.600 --> 27:47.120
because we're reasoning in terms of a two-dimensional function.

27:47.120 --> 27:48.160
All right.

27:48.160 --> 27:52.360
So it turns out that some masks are separable,

27:52.360 --> 27:55.720
which means that they can be written as the convolution

27:55.720 --> 27:58.360
of two sub-masks.

27:58.360 --> 28:02.360
Sometimes this is useful, because applying

28:02.360 --> 28:05.760
and exploiting associativity property,

28:05.760 --> 28:09.600
it's often cheaper to first apply f1 followed by f2,

28:09.600 --> 28:11.560
rather than applying f directly.

28:11.560 --> 28:13.560
So these are just some tricks that people use in order

28:13.560 --> 28:19.600
to speed up the image processing pipeline.

28:19.600 --> 28:24.680
So there is a particular case whereby

28:24.680 --> 28:28.720
a mask is presentable as an outer product of two vectors,

28:28.720 --> 28:31.160
which corresponds to two-dimensional convolution.

28:31.160 --> 28:35.160
So outer product will be the product of, say, this vector

28:35.160 --> 28:37.920
and this vector.

28:37.920 --> 28:43.240
Say, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1.

28:43.240 --> 28:46.040
What is the result of this outer product?

28:46.040 --> 28:47.960
f.

28:47.960 --> 28:51.040
Capital F, so basically the moving average,

28:51.040 --> 28:54.080
unnormalized moving average matrix.

28:54.080 --> 28:58.840
So why is, so we started with an f that was a matrix.

28:58.840 --> 29:02.360
We represented it as an outer product of two vectors.

29:02.360 --> 29:04.160
Why is that useful?

29:04.160 --> 29:09.320
So suppose that we want to again carry out the same operation.

29:13.880 --> 29:15.920
We have convolution.

29:15.920 --> 29:22.480
So in general, let the mask be m by m.

29:22.480 --> 29:24.920
And let the, I want to make sure I use the same notation,

29:24.920 --> 29:28.720
the image has size w times h.

29:28.720 --> 29:31.760
w is the width, the number of columns,

29:31.760 --> 29:34.880
h is the height, the number of rows.

29:34.880 --> 29:42.720
So if I apply the moving average mask with size m by m

29:42.720 --> 29:45.840
to an image with size w times h,

29:45.840 --> 29:49.280
how many operations do I have to carry out?

29:49.280 --> 29:59.480
So per pixel, I need to carry out m square multiplications.

29:59.480 --> 30:02.760
Is that right?

30:02.760 --> 30:05.280
Because I have to carry out one multiplication

30:05.280 --> 30:07.600
for each component of the mask.

30:07.600 --> 30:11.040
You're assuming that the mask has size m times m,

30:11.040 --> 30:13.320
which means that I have m square components.

30:13.320 --> 30:16.120
So per each pixel in the input image,

30:16.120 --> 30:19.920
I had to carry out m square multiplications.

30:19.920 --> 30:22.000
And then an addition.

30:22.000 --> 30:27.800
Since how many pixels do I have in the input image?

30:27.800 --> 30:30.080
W times h.

30:30.080 --> 30:36.600
So in total, I had to carry out m square w h operations.

30:36.600 --> 30:39.120
But now we are smarter and we recognize that actually

30:39.160 --> 30:44.080
the matrix with all ones are normalized.

30:44.080 --> 30:47.000
If it is normalized, then we would just add a scalar here.

30:47.000 --> 30:48.560
It wouldn't make any difference.

30:48.560 --> 30:52.080
It can be written as the outer product of two vectors.

30:52.080 --> 30:56.040
So let's first apply convolution of the input image with respect

30:56.040 --> 30:59.400
to this vector, which can be considered a mask.

30:59.400 --> 31:02.240
It's a trivial mask because there's only one row,

31:02.240 --> 31:03.680
but still it's a mask.

31:03.680 --> 31:07.240
And then we apply the convolution of the resulting,

31:07.640 --> 31:12.280
and then we consider a convolution of the resulting image with this mask.

31:12.280 --> 31:14.800
So when we first take the convolution with respect to this vector,

31:14.800 --> 31:20.080
how many operations do I need to carry out?

31:20.080 --> 31:25.080
Per each pixel, now I have a mask with three elements.

31:25.080 --> 31:27.560
So I need to carry out three operations.

31:27.560 --> 31:31.440
In general, with m elements, I have to carry out m operations per pixel.

31:31.440 --> 31:34.120
And again, I had to repeat this procedure,

31:34.120 --> 31:35.880
w times h times.

31:39.880 --> 31:46.520
And then I have to convolve the resulting image with this column vector,

31:46.520 --> 31:50.800
which entails mwh operations.

31:50.800 --> 31:55.600
So in total, I need to carry out two mwh operations.

31:55.600 --> 31:59.240
So in general, m square is much larger than 2m,

31:59.240 --> 32:03.400
which means that actually recognizing that in the case

32:03.400 --> 32:06.920
of the moving average filter, the filter is separable,

32:06.920 --> 32:10.200
would lead to dramatic computational savings.

32:10.200 --> 32:11.200
Yeah?

32:11.200 --> 32:16.080
But also, aren't all these filters based on some sort of localness?

32:16.080 --> 32:17.640
So m is not going to get too big, right?

32:17.640 --> 32:20.720
So this doesn't give too dramatic of a savings.

32:20.720 --> 32:23.120
Well, still, 3 by 3 is a very small thing.

32:23.120 --> 32:28.160
So it could be, say, the order of 5 or 6.

32:28.160 --> 32:33.280
So the difference between m square and 2m is seen.

32:33.280 --> 32:37.840
It's sizable, especially because you're multiplying by wh,

32:37.840 --> 32:39.760
which will be quite large.

32:39.760 --> 32:42.280
So even a factor of 10 or a factor of 50?

32:42.280 --> 32:43.880
Do you have 20 pixels to get a factor?

32:43.880 --> 32:47.320
Yeah, yeah.

32:47.320 --> 32:49.320
And it costs you nothing.

32:49.320 --> 32:52.200
So that is also the other thing, right?

32:52.200 --> 32:56.680
It's just that for this example, the convolution is equal to the other product.

32:56.680 --> 32:59.040
Yeah, yeah.

32:59.040 --> 33:01.560
No, so not all masks are separable.

33:01.560 --> 33:05.000
But it turns out that a lot of them are.

33:05.000 --> 33:11.040
In particular, besides the moving average, also the Gaussian smoothing is separable.

33:11.040 --> 33:19.600
And this is due to the fact that the Gaussian distribution can be broken into its x and

33:19.600 --> 33:22.380
y Gaussian distributions.

33:22.380 --> 33:28.640
So for most practical cases, we can use leverage separable masks.

33:28.640 --> 33:29.640
But you're right.

33:29.640 --> 33:35.640
You're right, you're right, you're right, you're right.

33:35.640 --> 33:40.560
So we talked about filters, spatial linear filters.

33:40.560 --> 33:46.960
We considered two main classes, correlation and convolution, which basically are brothers.

33:46.960 --> 33:49.200
One is just a flipped version of the other ones.

33:49.200 --> 33:54.960
We consider, as example, smoothing filters out of the moving average of the Gaussian

33:54.960 --> 33:56.520
smoothing filter.

33:56.520 --> 34:03.760
But a very, very important additional example of a linear filter is the one whereby the

34:03.760 --> 34:08.200
mask implements the notion of a derivative of an image.

34:08.200 --> 34:10.880
Now, how do we define the derivative of an image?

34:10.880 --> 34:19.220
Since an image is a discrete function, meaning that the domain is basically the rows and

34:19.220 --> 34:25.280
columns of an image, so it's a discrete domain, the output is discrete as well at the quantized

34:25.280 --> 34:31.120
levels of intensity, we cannot really use the standard notion of derivative for the

34:31.120 --> 34:32.940
council calculus.

34:32.940 --> 34:36.120
We have to consider a discrete version of a derivative.

34:36.120 --> 34:45.320
Now in the case of a discrete definition of a derivative of a function, we have actually

34:45.320 --> 34:51.400
quite a bit of freedom as we can define the derivative of a discrete function.

34:51.400 --> 35:01.640
Another possible way is to define the derivative of an image with respect to X, X means in

35:01.640 --> 35:11.480
the horizontal component, so the column-wise component, as, actually sorry, it means in

35:11.480 --> 35:21.360
the row-wise component, E I of X plus Y Y minus I X minus 1 I.

35:21.360 --> 35:37.160
So basically if we have our image, so this is our X Y, say that this is 4, this is 8,

35:37.160 --> 35:44.600
this is 10, what is the derivative in the row-wise direction?

35:44.600 --> 35:54.600
Two, yeah, no it's not meant to be a tricky question, just two.

35:54.600 --> 36:02.220
So then in the output image at the corresponding X Y location, I will put two.

36:02.220 --> 36:08.140
Which makes sense, the derivative encodes the notion of change in a signal.

36:08.140 --> 36:14.100
Here we cannot really reason in terms of differential increments, but still we can reason in terms

36:14.100 --> 36:16.620
of the finite differences.

36:16.620 --> 36:21.340
So the derivative of an image is really defined as a finite difference in the pixel space.

36:21.340 --> 36:25.860
You say there's multiple ways to do it, but is there a reason we consider we're looking

36:25.860 --> 36:29.140
at the indices of either side versus the one we're at?

36:29.140 --> 36:36.820
Yeah, so another way is to consider one-sided derivatives, like whereby you could define

36:36.820 --> 36:43.860
this as I X I X plus 1 Y minus I X Y.

36:43.860 --> 36:51.540
So this is the one-sided derivative, or also Y X Y minus Y X minus 1 Y, it will be up or

36:51.540 --> 36:52.540
down.

36:52.540 --> 37:01.060
In reality this is generally a little bit more robust because it considers values in

37:01.060 --> 37:07.860
the neighborhood, both sides of the neighborhood of the pixel equation.

37:07.860 --> 37:16.180
But this prompts the question about can we consider even more robust definition of a

37:16.180 --> 37:21.180
derivative or a computational derivative, which leads to the so-called Sobel masks that

37:21.180 --> 37:25.960
are really the ones that are used in computer vision to take derivatives.

37:25.960 --> 37:33.640
The point with the Sobel mask is try to provide a more robust definition of derivative by

37:33.640 --> 37:40.600
taking into account not just the difference with respect to a single column, so column

37:40.600 --> 37:48.060
Y, but also the differences, discrete differences in neighboring columns, and then take an average

37:48.060 --> 37:52.720
of those differences in an effort to have something that is more robust to outliers

37:52.720 --> 37:55.680
noise and so on.

37:55.680 --> 38:06.040
So for example, on the X direction, Sobel consists of a three by three mask where we

38:06.040 --> 38:12.800
take derivative in three neighboring directions.

38:12.800 --> 38:20.360
We give more weight to the one where the pixel equation lies and less weight to the rows

38:20.360 --> 38:25.300
which are in the neighborhoods of that pixel, similar to what we do with the Gaussian smoothing

38:25.300 --> 38:26.300
mask.

38:26.300 --> 38:34.600
Is there a reason why all of these filters are three by three or you can choose?

38:34.600 --> 38:35.600
Usually they're larger.

38:35.600 --> 38:40.740
I do three by three simply because it's easier to print it, easier to explain it, and when

38:40.740 --> 38:43.100
you do computation by hand it's easier.

38:43.100 --> 38:47.680
But no, in general it's left a little bit up to the user to decide what is the width

38:47.680 --> 38:50.280
of the mask.

38:50.280 --> 38:53.280
That's a good question.

38:53.280 --> 39:01.160
So view Sobel masks as just a more robust implementation of the two side discrete difference

39:01.160 --> 39:03.740
definition of the derivative of a mask.

39:03.740 --> 39:07.600
But the key point is that basically you take differences of intensities.

39:07.600 --> 39:13.040
So what's the direction of the X orientation?

39:13.040 --> 39:17.920
So X here, this will be the row.

39:17.920 --> 39:21.640
So it will be in the vertical direction.

39:21.640 --> 39:29.120
Here it will be in the Y, so this will be in the column wise direction.

39:29.120 --> 39:37.520
Keep in mind that we're doing a convolution here, so these are mirrored.

39:37.520 --> 39:47.400
So the filters can also be used for detecting similarities.

39:47.400 --> 39:54.140
In particular similarities between two images in order, for example, to detect correspondences

39:54.140 --> 39:56.800
that are useful for structural motion.

39:56.800 --> 40:04.320
Two popular ways of detecting similarities are to take differences in intensity values

40:04.320 --> 40:10.180
in the neighborhood of each pixel and then take the absolute difference or take the square

40:10.180 --> 40:11.180
of the difference.

40:11.180 --> 40:14.460
The first approach is referred to as sum of absolute differences.

40:14.460 --> 40:17.180
The second one is referred to as sum of square differences.

40:17.180 --> 40:22.700
They're just two definitions.

40:22.700 --> 40:23.700
All right.

40:23.700 --> 40:27.340
I think this is good background in terms of filtering.

40:27.340 --> 40:29.500
Now how do we make filtering useful?

40:29.500 --> 40:36.700
Well, as I said, the second step in the image processing pipeline entails detecting local

40:36.700 --> 40:46.500
features, that is the patterns that differ from the immediate neighborhood, such as an

40:46.500 --> 40:50.380
edge, a corner, a blob, and so on and so forth.

40:50.380 --> 40:55.780
In particular in this class, we will focus on edge detectors and corner detectors.

40:55.780 --> 41:00.180
Edge detectors will be useful because they will allow us to detect lines that we will

41:00.180 --> 41:08.460
use as a geometric primitives in order to build a localization module for your robot.

41:08.460 --> 41:11.860
Now why are detectors descriptors useful?

41:11.860 --> 41:16.020
Well, first of all for stereo reconstruction, as already mentioned.

41:16.020 --> 41:23.380
Second of all, they can be useful in order to reconstruct transformation and hence they're

41:23.380 --> 41:27.260
very related to the notion of structure for motion, if you were in the lecture on Friday.

41:27.660 --> 41:32.860
Beyond robotics, they can be used for other computer vision tasks such as panorama stitching.

41:32.860 --> 41:38.180
You take the images of a mountain from two different perspectives and then you want to

41:38.180 --> 41:41.980
stitch them in order to have a broader view.

41:41.980 --> 41:47.540
You detect points that correspond to each other and then you use them as anchors in

41:47.540 --> 41:54.740
order to stitch the two images or even to detect objects, as we will see next time.

41:54.740 --> 42:04.620
So as I said, the first feature that we want to detect is an edge.

42:04.620 --> 42:13.540
An edge is defined as a region of an image where you have a stark difference in intensity,

42:13.540 --> 42:17.220
saying from white to black or from black to white.

42:17.220 --> 42:23.860
Now for you, I mean for the human eye, detecting an edge is relatively simple, but for computer

42:23.860 --> 42:27.500
vision, it could be quite tricky.

42:27.500 --> 42:33.140
In particular, from a mathematical standpoint, an edge is a region of the environment whereby

42:33.140 --> 42:40.980
you have a large difference, a large value for the first order derivative and a very

42:40.980 --> 42:43.940
small value for the second order derivative.

42:43.940 --> 42:50.900
A very large value for the first order derivative is obvious because you have this discrete

42:50.900 --> 42:53.060
jump in intensities.

42:53.060 --> 42:59.420
The reason for the second order derivative to be small will be seen in a second.

42:59.420 --> 43:07.300
The point is to exploit this fact that an edge corresponds to a dramatic difference

43:07.300 --> 43:10.980
in intensity and hence high value of the derivative in order to detect them.

43:10.980 --> 43:17.660
So in particular, we will implement a derivative filter and then we apply it to the input image.

43:17.660 --> 43:22.700
We get an output image that corresponds to the derivatives, so the differences in intensities.

43:22.700 --> 43:28.540
And then we say that there is an edge where those derivatives are very large.

43:28.540 --> 43:30.220
So it's pretty simple.

43:30.220 --> 43:33.500
Make sense?

43:33.500 --> 43:40.540
So there are a number of criteria for having a good edge detection algorithm.

43:40.540 --> 43:46.780
First of all, we want to have accuracy, meaning that we want to minimize a false positive

43:46.780 --> 43:53.260
detecting an edge when actually there is no edge, or false negatives, missing real edges.

43:53.260 --> 44:00.780
Localization, we want to make sure that once we detect an edge, we also localize that edge

44:00.780 --> 44:04.540
in the same position where that edge really is.

44:04.540 --> 44:09.100
And third, we want to have single response, so we want to make sure that one edge is detected

44:09.100 --> 44:13.900
as a single edge as opposed to multiple edges.

44:13.900 --> 44:18.140
Now the approach entails basically two steps that are already alluded to.

44:18.140 --> 44:24.580
First, we will apply a smoothing step in order to remove noise, and then we will take a derivative

44:24.580 --> 44:26.460
with respect to the smooth image.

44:26.460 --> 44:33.220
Eventually, we realize that the entire procedure encompasses a convolution.

44:33.220 --> 44:38.700
So by using the associativity property, we will differentiate the smoothing filter and

44:38.700 --> 44:42.940
apply the resulting mask to the target image.

44:42.940 --> 44:48.460
So to make things concrete, let's assume that we have just a one-dimensional image.

44:48.460 --> 45:01.540
So basically, we have an image that is just a row vector with intensities, say 50, 70,

45:01.540 --> 45:12.940
in this case it would be 50, 50, 50, and then all of a sudden 120, 120, and so on.

45:12.940 --> 45:21.380
So basically, we have a one-dimensional image which corresponds to just a row, and we have

45:21.380 --> 45:25.540
low intensity and a discrete jump, and then we have high intensity.

45:25.540 --> 45:30.460
But in general, in the intensity values, we have some noise.

45:30.460 --> 45:38.500
It's not that you have 50, 50, 50, but maybe you have 51, you have 48, 52, and so on.

45:38.500 --> 45:43.620
So if you were to differentiate this image directly by applying the finite difference

45:43.620 --> 45:48.980
scheme that I mentioned before, this will be a typical output.

45:48.980 --> 45:53.380
X here denotes the index of the vector.

45:53.380 --> 45:55.740
Y is the value of the intensity.

45:55.740 --> 46:01.220
As you can see, by just looking at the output of the differentiated image, it's impossible

46:01.220 --> 46:04.820
to see where the edge really lies.

46:04.820 --> 46:06.420
Why is it the case?

46:06.420 --> 46:12.680
It's that since we had all this noise, this noise, by taking the derivative, gets amplified.

46:12.680 --> 46:17.340
So basically, the differences that you can take here per pixel are very similar to the

46:17.340 --> 46:21.580
differences that you can take here per pixel, and so what you get as an output is something

46:21.580 --> 46:26.140
that is not usable.

46:26.140 --> 46:31.100
So what you do is actually to filter the image out.

46:31.100 --> 46:39.340
So again, we have an input image, which entails this step in intensity, but it's a bit noisy.

46:39.340 --> 46:50.720
So what we do is to consider a Gaussian smoothing mask that, as you remember, averages the pixels

46:50.720 --> 46:56.520
in the neighborhood, but gives more weight to the pixel where the mask is centered at,

46:56.520 --> 47:01.340
and then less weight to the tail of the mask.

47:01.340 --> 47:06.720
And of course, the idea is that you apply the average operation here, then you move

47:06.720 --> 47:09.720
one step to the right, and then you keep going.

47:09.720 --> 47:15.400
The result will be the smooth image here at the bottom, which is the result of applying

47:15.400 --> 47:21.160
the smoothing Gaussian kernel or mask with the input image.

47:21.160 --> 47:31.180
At this point, you apply the derivative filter, and then what you get is magically one blimp.

47:31.180 --> 47:37.880
So one patch of the image where you have a high gradient, and here the second derivative

47:37.880 --> 47:40.880
is zero.

47:40.880 --> 47:44.820
Because first you go up and then you go down, so that's why you have.

47:44.820 --> 47:48.980
So now it will be very easy to detect where an edge is.

47:48.980 --> 47:55.020
You just look at the peak of the derivative, and that's where the edge is, which corresponds

47:55.020 --> 47:57.740
more or less where the actual edge really is.

47:57.740 --> 48:02.140
Does that make sense?

48:02.140 --> 48:03.900
Good.

48:03.900 --> 48:10.940
So as I said before, what we've done will work fine, but for computational reason, it's

48:10.940 --> 48:17.140
easier to exploit the property of a convolution that says that the convolution is associative.

48:17.140 --> 48:22.780
Which means that instead of first moving the image and then taking the derivative of the

48:22.780 --> 48:30.160
image, what we can do is to first take the derivative of the smoothing mask and then

48:30.160 --> 48:36.220
apply the resulting mask that I refer to as a G prime to the input image.

48:36.220 --> 48:43.140
The advantage is that we only do the convolution with a big two by two matrix, the image only

48:43.140 --> 48:44.140
once here.

48:44.140 --> 48:46.140
Instead of here, you would do it twice.

48:46.140 --> 48:52.860
When you do the derivative of the mask, do you need to pad the mask with zeros?

48:52.860 --> 48:53.860
Yeah.

48:53.860 --> 49:01.700
Every time you have to do, you have to account for the multiple conditions, yes.

49:01.700 --> 49:05.780
So here, you see how here actually the width of the mask is quite large.

49:05.780 --> 49:09.220
So we have about 400 values.

49:09.220 --> 49:14.700
So remember what the smoothing Gaussian mask was.

49:14.700 --> 49:16.180
It's just a Gaussian, just a peak.

49:16.180 --> 49:18.980
Of course, it's discretized.

49:18.980 --> 49:20.100
So what is the derivative?

49:20.100 --> 49:23.480
Here the derivative is positive and here the derivative is negative.

49:23.480 --> 49:29.260
So how does this derivative of the smoothing Gaussian mask look like?

49:29.260 --> 49:31.220
Like this.

49:31.220 --> 49:34.860
First the derivative is positive and then the derivative is negative.

49:34.860 --> 49:44.700
So then if you apply the derivative, differentiated Gaussian filter to the original input, we

49:44.700 --> 49:46.300
get the same output.

49:46.300 --> 49:53.100
Isn't that neat that we do this and what's that?

49:53.100 --> 49:57.520
Now in reality, what we care about are the two-dimensional images.

49:57.520 --> 50:04.780
So what we had to do is to smooth the image in two dimensions.

50:04.780 --> 50:11.780
So basically, accounting for the two-dimensional Gaussian smoothing mask, the one that was

50:11.780 --> 50:16.700
here, this one here, this is two-dimensional.

50:16.700 --> 50:22.660
Basically, you can view it as a Gaussian in two dimensions.

50:22.660 --> 50:32.620
And now you have to take derivatives in two directions, horizontal and vertical direction.

50:32.620 --> 50:37.300
As you were saying, you have to account for variations not just with respect to the rows

50:37.300 --> 50:41.700
or the columns, but with respect to both the rows and the columns, because the edge obviously

50:41.700 --> 50:47.880
can be oriented in any possible way.

50:47.880 --> 50:50.860
So this is just a straightforward generalization of what we did before.

50:50.860 --> 50:58.260
Again, the idea is that instead of performing the smoothing of the image, we first take

50:58.260 --> 51:02.900
the derivative of the smoothing mask and then we convert that to the input image to

51:02.900 --> 51:06.240
save on operations.

51:06.240 --> 51:19.140
So you do that and you get as an output image two images, one which encodes the derivatives

51:19.140 --> 51:27.460
in the x direction and the other one that encodes the derivative in the y direction.

51:27.660 --> 51:36.460
And pixel by pixels, you take the square values and you sum them, the result is still an image.

51:36.460 --> 51:46.020
And then you look at those pixels in the resulting image that has a maximum value.

51:46.020 --> 51:50.180
There is a little bit more to that, but we will see that in the next slide.

51:50.180 --> 51:56.540
Does it make sense?

51:56.540 --> 52:02.020
So this is in particular how the Gaussian filter looks like in two dimensions.

52:02.020 --> 52:07.060
When you take the derivative with respect to the x direction, again, here it's going

52:07.060 --> 52:12.920
up, so the derivative is positive, here it's going down, so that's why you have a ballet.

52:12.920 --> 52:18.160
And then when you do the y, it will be in the orthogonal direction.

52:18.160 --> 52:24.700
So this is the filter that you will have here and the one that is rotated by 90 degrees

52:24.700 --> 52:26.700
is the filter that you have here.

52:26.700 --> 52:30.700
How does this work for diagonal edges?

52:30.700 --> 52:32.700
Is it the same thing or?

52:32.700 --> 52:36.300
Yes, because that is accounted for here.

52:36.300 --> 52:42.700
That's why at the end you have to take basically the square root of the squares of both.

52:42.700 --> 52:46.660
All right, so let's see it in practice.

52:46.660 --> 52:50.340
So let's get the image of this person first.

52:50.340 --> 52:56.880
We use the differentiated Gaussian filter.

52:56.880 --> 53:01.240
This will be the output with respect to y, that will be the output with respect to x.

53:01.240 --> 53:07.180
These are the two images, sx and sy, that I mentioned here.

53:07.180 --> 53:13.380
At this point, I combine them by taking the square root of sx squared plus sy squared.

53:13.380 --> 53:17.760
That is pixel per pixel, so that's why as an output I still get an image.

53:17.760 --> 53:27.440
I only select those pixels such that the value is greater than a certain threshold,

53:27.440 --> 53:28.440
which is a designer choice.

53:28.440 --> 53:31.480
Everything else is set equal to zero.

53:31.480 --> 53:38.400
And finally, in order to have a crisp result, I apply a last step, which is the non-maxima

53:38.400 --> 53:39.400
suppression.

53:39.400 --> 53:45.760
Basically, I look in each neighborhood and for each neighborhood, I only consider the

53:45.760 --> 53:52.120
points that are at a maximum, in order to have basically lines that are thinner.

53:52.120 --> 53:53.120
Yeah.

53:53.120 --> 53:59.560
Is this value h that we're dropping out all the things that are below a certain threshold,

53:59.560 --> 54:03.080
is that sort of what you set on like an image slider for image trace?

54:03.080 --> 54:13.040
For example, yes, because basically if you make h very high, then you don't get very

54:13.040 --> 54:14.040
much.

54:14.040 --> 54:19.560
On the other hand, you don't classify as an edge, something that is not an edge.

54:19.560 --> 54:23.880
If you say you're making very low, then there is the risk that you're classifying as an

54:23.880 --> 54:25.420
edge, something that is not an edge.

54:25.420 --> 54:29.960
So it's really a threshold that you get, that you tune in order to find a good trade-off

54:29.960 --> 54:33.720
between false positives and false negatives.

54:33.720 --> 54:36.120
So that is worth a bit of tuning.

54:36.120 --> 54:39.120
In any case, does the entire process pipeline make sense?

54:49.720 --> 54:52.840
The concept, as I said, is quite simple.

54:52.840 --> 55:01.200
You just take this smoothing in order to remove noise, then you take differences in y direction

55:01.200 --> 55:05.760
and x direction, and then you have two images.

55:05.760 --> 55:10.040
For each pixel, you have the differences in one of the two directions.

55:10.040 --> 55:14.480
Then you combine them by basically summing them by making sure that you square this one,

55:14.480 --> 55:18.440
you square the other one, you take the square root, that will be the output.

55:18.440 --> 55:24.920
And then you do a little bit of post-processing in order to retain only those gradient information

55:24.920 --> 55:30.400
that passes a threshold test in order to find good trade between false positives and

55:30.400 --> 55:31.400
false negatives.

55:31.400 --> 55:34.920
All right, so it was for edges.

55:34.920 --> 55:38.100
Let's talk about corners.

55:38.100 --> 55:45.720
So corners are also very useful features, especially for matching, for example, for

55:45.720 --> 55:46.720
stereo vision.

55:46.720 --> 55:51.400
Similar to edges, we have a number of criteria that we would like for a corner detector to

55:51.400 --> 55:57.320
have, like repeatability, same feature can be found in multiple images despite geometric

55:57.320 --> 56:03.120
and photometric transformations, and distinctiveness, meaning that information carried by patch

56:03.120 --> 56:08.480
surrounding the future should be as distinctive as possible in order to make sure, again,

56:08.480 --> 56:14.640
to make sure that you have consistent matching pairs across images.

56:14.640 --> 56:24.920
In particular, in terms of repeatability, if you pick some corners here, then for whatever

56:24.920 --> 56:33.120
reason you cannot pick on an image with slightly different pose, then you cannot find any matching.

56:33.120 --> 56:38.760
So here is a case where this corner was not detected.

56:38.760 --> 56:44.200
Actually none of these corners were detected, and other corners were detected.

56:44.200 --> 56:48.280
And now we cannot find the matchings anymore, which means that we can do anything in terms

56:48.280 --> 56:49.700
of processing these two images.

56:49.700 --> 56:52.880
So this will be bad performance.

56:52.880 --> 56:56.360
The other one is to be distinctive.

56:56.360 --> 57:08.160
So if I detect this corner here, but I cannot describe it in a way that is distinctive enough

57:08.160 --> 57:12.120
in order to understand whether this corner really corresponds to this one, this one,

57:12.120 --> 57:20.740
this one, and this one, then again, I cannot do any sort of matching.

57:20.740 --> 57:24.260
So do you have any intuition about how we could detect a corner?

57:24.260 --> 57:29.540
So we said that an edge corresponds to a region of an image, whereby we have a sudden change

57:29.540 --> 57:32.040
in intensity in one direction.

57:32.040 --> 57:33.880
Exactly.

57:33.880 --> 57:48.980
So a corner is basically defined as part of the image where you have changes in all directions.

57:48.980 --> 57:55.840
And you exploit this definition in order to design a corner detector.

57:55.840 --> 58:02.860
I'm not going to go into the details of how to design a corner detector, but by now you

58:02.860 --> 58:06.520
get corner detectors already implemented for you in visual libraries.

58:06.520 --> 58:09.180
But I just want to give an intuition.

58:09.180 --> 58:19.100
The idea is to take a window, say a mask, and then you slide it around each pixel.

58:19.100 --> 58:25.620
So if you are in a region that is flat, meaning the intensity is uniform, if you shake this

58:25.620 --> 58:28.820
window around, the response is the same.

58:28.820 --> 58:32.560
So here there is not an edge nor a corner.

58:32.560 --> 58:40.120
If you said that you're close to an edge and you move the window up and down, then

58:40.120 --> 58:41.740
the response is the same.

58:41.740 --> 58:48.340
But when you move it in the orthogonal direction, then you have this drastic change in intensity,

58:48.340 --> 58:50.580
which means that you have an edge.

58:50.580 --> 58:55.680
If you're here, no matter how you move the window, you have a drastic change in intensity,

58:55.680 --> 58:57.860
which means that you have a corner.

58:57.860 --> 59:04.420
That's basically what a corner detector exploits in order to detect a corner.

59:04.420 --> 59:12.020
Now one very popular corner detector is the so-called hardest detector that exploits this

59:12.020 --> 59:20.000
notion that if you move a window locally in all directions and you have changes of intensity

59:20.000 --> 59:22.780
in all directions, then you have a corner.

59:22.780 --> 59:25.340
So that detector is referred to as a hardest detector.

59:25.820 --> 59:29.700
What a hardest detector does is really to translate this notion of shaking around the

59:29.700 --> 59:40.260
window into a mathematical test that essentially entails taking some derivatives and then processing

59:40.260 --> 59:47.920
those derivatives through an uninvited composition in order to find the corners.

59:47.920 --> 59:59.060
So here you can see how the hardest detector detects corners with remarkable repeatability,

59:59.060 --> 01:00:09.440
even though the image is taken from a very different perspective and there are significant

01:00:09.440 --> 01:00:10.440
intensity differences.

01:00:10.440 --> 01:00:16.080
This is much more gray than this one.

01:00:16.080 --> 01:00:21.320
In particular one can show, and this is due to the fact that the hardest detector reasons

01:00:21.320 --> 01:00:27.840
in terms of eigenvalues, that the hardest detector is invariant to rotation.

01:00:27.840 --> 01:00:34.900
So if you rotate the image, the hardest detector will pick up the same corners, which is great.

01:00:34.900 --> 01:00:38.380
And it's also invariant to intensity changes.

01:00:38.380 --> 01:00:45.140
So if you uniformly scale your image by the same quantity, so you add 50 to all pixels,

01:00:45.140 --> 01:00:53.660
the hardest detector will pick the same corners, which again is great.

01:00:53.660 --> 01:00:58.860
The problem though is that the hardest detector is not invariant to scale changes.

01:00:58.860 --> 01:01:00.140
This is annoying.

01:01:00.140 --> 01:01:05.020
So meaning that if I move away from an object, there is a danger that the hardest detector

01:01:05.020 --> 01:01:10.780
does not pick the same corners that they picked with the previous image.

01:01:10.780 --> 01:01:14.820
The reason why is that the case is that since the hardest detector uses a window with a

01:01:14.820 --> 01:01:22.360
fixed size, at some scale something may look like a corner, but at another scale the very

01:01:22.360 --> 01:01:32.940
same feature, but the very same geometric entity in the image may look like an edge.

01:01:32.940 --> 01:01:37.700
So for example, let's say that this is the original image and you have this line.

01:01:37.700 --> 01:01:43.460
You plan your window, you move it around, in all directions you have changes, we classify

01:01:43.460 --> 01:01:44.460
it as a corner.

01:01:44.460 --> 01:01:50.060
Now we get closer to the line, we apply this window with the same size.

01:01:50.060 --> 01:01:54.980
So now, as I move the window around, I get change in this direction, but not so much

01:01:54.980 --> 01:01:55.980
in this direction.

01:01:55.980 --> 01:02:04.060
So now I classify this as an edge, which is disastrous.

01:02:04.060 --> 01:02:08.800
Because if you do it for all corners, then you don't have any matching anymore.

01:02:08.800 --> 01:02:17.800
And so stereo vision and then, for example, for robots, visual odometry, whereby you use

01:02:17.800 --> 01:02:24.440
sequences of images in order to understand how much you have moved, can't be done anymore.

01:02:24.440 --> 01:02:30.500
So this triggered a lot of research on how to find detectors that not only are rotation

01:02:30.500 --> 01:02:39.900
invariant and intensity invariant, but they're also invariant to scale changes.

01:02:39.900 --> 01:02:43.740
Some of the very popular ones are an extension of the Harris detector, referred to as Harris

01:02:43.740 --> 01:02:50.700
Laplacian, or the SIFT detector, which is also very, very popular.

01:02:50.700 --> 01:02:55.340
We won't go into more details about detectors, this is not really a class only on computer

01:02:55.340 --> 01:03:00.060
vision, but I just want to make sure that you know the relevant terms in computer vision

01:03:00.060 --> 01:03:03.980
for the main detectors, for example, SIFT.

01:03:03.980 --> 01:03:07.460
So far we've talked about detectors.

01:03:07.460 --> 01:03:11.100
Once you have detected a feature, you want to make sure that you describe it so that

01:03:11.100 --> 01:03:16.780
you can associate it with the same feature in another image.

01:03:16.780 --> 01:03:19.140
That's the role of a descriptor.

01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:24.740
Descriptor describe the key points on features so that we can compare them across images.

01:03:24.740 --> 01:03:35.020
The most unique way of coding a descriptor is just to detect a feature and then use as

01:03:35.020 --> 01:03:47.060
descriptor the pixel intensities in the neighborhood of that feature.

01:03:47.060 --> 01:03:53.900
Now today's descriptor is that we associate with a given key point an n by m window of

01:03:53.900 --> 01:03:56.340
pixel intensities centered at that key point.

01:03:56.340 --> 01:04:03.580
For example, here we have this blob, we consider as descriptor just the intensities of the

01:04:03.580 --> 01:04:06.060
neighboring pixels.

01:04:06.060 --> 01:04:15.420
Now the main drawback of this descriptor is that it's very sensitive to scale.

01:04:15.420 --> 01:04:22.940
So if we get closer to this lump, then everything in the neighborhood will look very bright.

01:04:22.940 --> 01:04:26.980
And then again, that is not a very good way of matching.

01:04:26.980 --> 01:04:33.080
So it's very sensitive to scale, it's sensitive to poles, and it's also poorly distinctive.

01:04:33.080 --> 01:04:36.060
So there are much better descriptors out there.

01:04:36.060 --> 01:04:38.780
Again, SIFT is one of the more prominent ones.

01:04:38.780 --> 01:04:41.180
SIFT is both a detector and a descriptor.

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:50.900
So SIFT is a detector and a descriptor, and as a descriptor, essentially relies on the

01:04:50.900 --> 01:04:56.020
local distribution of image gradients around key points.

01:04:56.020 --> 01:05:02.520
It takes many, many gradients, and indeed, the descriptor is a 128-dimensional vector.

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:10.180
So actually, the SIFT detector and descriptor is a very heavy weight descriptor.

01:05:10.180 --> 01:05:11.180
So that's a disadvantage.

01:05:11.180 --> 01:05:17.380
The advantage, though, is that it has a number of very appealing properties in terms of scaling

01:05:17.380 --> 01:05:20.460
variance and the variance of rotation and so on.

01:05:20.460 --> 01:05:25.380
There are many others out there, like orb risk.

01:05:25.380 --> 01:05:32.020
For my work on visual geometry for navigation, asteroids for robots, I was using, for example,

01:05:32.020 --> 01:05:33.020
orb.

01:05:33.020 --> 01:05:41.280
You have very robust situations whereby you have dramatic changes in intensity.

01:05:41.280 --> 01:05:46.400
So you're basically jumping on an asteroid, and you have a pitch black of the universe,

01:05:46.400 --> 01:05:54.860
and then you have the very shiny white reflection of the sand.

01:05:54.860 --> 01:05:58.900
And as you bounce, you have this pitch black or very bright white.

01:05:58.900 --> 01:06:02.300
So you need to have a detector that is very robust with respect to these sudden changes

01:06:02.340 --> 01:06:03.340
in intensity.

01:06:03.340 --> 01:06:05.620
So orb works very well for us.

01:06:05.620 --> 01:06:13.220
I mean, the point is that if in the future you need to reason at a level of detectors

01:06:13.220 --> 01:06:16.420
and descriptors, there are a bunch out there.

01:06:16.420 --> 01:06:20.340
Most of them are already implemented in visual libraries.

01:06:20.340 --> 01:06:23.460
So what you would do is first look at the literature about what people say in terms

01:06:23.460 --> 01:06:25.680
of advantages and disadvantages.

01:06:25.680 --> 01:06:32.500
But most importantly, you would try them all and see which ones work best.

01:06:32.500 --> 01:06:43.120
Now since 2010, 2012, a new paradigm has emerged in computer vision that relies on neural networks,

01:06:43.120 --> 01:06:45.900
in particular in convolutional neural networks.

01:06:45.900 --> 01:06:56.140
This is a completely different paradigm that in a nutshell implements the same functionalities

01:06:56.140 --> 01:07:03.900
that we described before in terms of filtering, in terms of detecting features, describing

01:07:03.900 --> 01:07:05.500
features, and so on.

01:07:05.500 --> 01:07:10.540
But it does so in a completely different computational paradigm, which is the paradigm of neural

01:07:10.540 --> 01:07:11.960
networks.

01:07:11.960 --> 01:07:15.840
We won't be covering neural networks in this class, so if you're interested, then you

01:07:15.840 --> 01:07:23.680
should take 274B, so principal robot autonomy 2, whereby the first module will be about

01:07:23.680 --> 01:07:28.400
learning-based and neural network-based vision and control.

01:07:28.400 --> 01:07:33.880
And in particular, we have one week where we will discuss how neural nets can be used

01:07:33.880 --> 01:07:39.440
to do a lot of the tasks that I explained before.

01:07:39.440 --> 01:07:45.320
And in particular, what they do very well is the fourth task, that of semantic understanding.

01:07:45.320 --> 01:07:50.780
For example, detecting images, putting bounding box around objects, and so on, to the point

01:07:50.780 --> 01:07:56.120
that neural nets have become the de facto standard for computer vision applications

01:07:56.120 --> 01:07:57.120
in robot autonomy.

01:07:57.120 --> 01:08:04.500
The disadvantage, well, first of all, they need a lot of data to train this more learning

01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:09.260
procedure as opposed to mathematical computational procedure.

01:08:09.260 --> 01:08:11.620
But that is not so much of a problem.

01:08:12.020 --> 01:08:17.100
Right now, there are very large data sets to train neural nets.

01:08:17.100 --> 01:08:26.220
But the problem is that sometimes it's very hard to understand how exactly neural nets

01:08:26.220 --> 01:08:28.580
really work after they have been trained.

01:08:28.580 --> 01:08:33.740
For example, you can see how in the first layers, you can see that, so these are basically

01:08:33.740 --> 01:08:37.580
the visualized initial layers of a CNN.

01:08:37.580 --> 01:08:42.060
You can see they can be interpreted as a mask, whereby, for example, here it looks

01:08:42.060 --> 01:08:46.900
like you're taking differences, and then you're taking energies for a small thing.

01:08:46.900 --> 01:08:54.620
So these basically are masks that are learned from data in order to classify objects and

01:08:54.620 --> 01:08:56.180
so on.

01:08:56.180 --> 01:08:57.180
But they're not super interpretable.

01:08:57.180 --> 01:09:00.120
I mean, sometimes they are, sometimes they're not.

01:09:00.120 --> 01:09:06.380
To the point that it's an open question of how much one can trust a neural network application.

01:09:06.380 --> 01:09:10.460
If I tell you that in order to detect an edge, I use a Gaussian filter and a derivative

01:09:10.460 --> 01:09:12.780
filter, you know exactly what they're doing.

01:09:12.780 --> 01:09:14.360
Here it's a little bit less clear.

01:09:14.360 --> 01:09:22.060
So now you're in an airplane that is trying to detect other objects on the runway, and

01:09:22.060 --> 01:09:27.580
it's based on a neural net, and you know exactly what are the computations that a neural net

01:09:27.580 --> 01:09:29.960
is doing in order to detect other objects.

01:09:29.960 --> 01:09:35.660
So the question is, am I relaxed, I sit back and relax, or am I going to be concerned?

01:09:35.660 --> 01:09:42.200
To the point that, for example, a number of agencies in the United States such as NASA

01:09:42.200 --> 01:09:49.260
and DARPA have specific programs in order to improve the interpretability of neural

01:09:49.260 --> 01:09:53.920
net pipelines for visual processing and for control.

01:09:53.920 --> 01:09:59.720
At the same time, try to reason in terms of assurances of properties that this system

01:09:59.720 --> 01:10:00.720
has.

01:10:00.720 --> 01:10:04.860
For example, I work on a program with DARPA called Assure Autonomy, where the problem

01:10:04.860 --> 01:10:06.100
is very simple.

01:10:06.100 --> 01:10:12.660
So the problem is that you have a plane that is maneuvering on the tarmac on the runway.

01:10:12.660 --> 01:10:17.380
It's trying to align itself with the center lane in order to take off.

01:10:17.380 --> 01:10:24.000
So it's relying on a camera in order to detect the center lane for the runway, and then on

01:10:24.000 --> 01:10:29.060
a simple proportional derivative controller in order to make sure that the airplane is

01:10:29.060 --> 01:10:30.180
aligned.

01:10:30.180 --> 01:10:35.080
So you have as an input an image, as an output you have steering control for the front wheel.

01:10:35.080 --> 01:10:38.340
In principle, with the classical approach, this is super easy.

01:10:38.340 --> 01:10:43.420
You just put the edge detectors that I show you today, they're trivial.

01:10:43.420 --> 01:10:47.500
On the other hand, if you use a neural net, the question is, how do you ensure that from

01:10:47.500 --> 01:10:52.520
time to time the neural net doesn't give a crazy output, maybe because there is some

01:10:52.520 --> 01:10:54.220
snow or some reflection or something?

01:10:54.220 --> 01:10:55.700
What type of assurances you can give?

01:10:55.700 --> 01:10:57.800
Well, this is a very active problem of research.

01:10:57.800 --> 01:11:01.600
So if you are interested in this kind of research, let me know.

01:11:01.600 --> 01:11:02.640
All right.

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:07.520
So these are all I wanted to say in terms of filtering, detection, and description.

01:11:07.520 --> 01:11:12.760
Obviously, as it is usual for all the other topics in this class, there is more to it.

01:11:12.760 --> 01:11:16.060
I gave you some readings at the beginning of the lecture.

01:11:16.060 --> 01:11:18.240
You can ask me if you're interested in getting deeper.

01:11:18.240 --> 01:11:22.200
Here, I just wanted to give you a general idea of the first three steps.

01:11:22.200 --> 01:11:30.500
And on Thursday, we will look at this type of extracting more large-scale features that

01:11:30.500 --> 01:11:37.320
might have also semantic meaning in order to A, inform localization and B, inform decision

01:11:37.320 --> 01:11:39.320
making.

01:11:39.320 --> 01:11:40.680
So I'll see you on Thursday.

